WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.860
A motor drive switches power electronics.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:08.200
If its processor computes one wrong value, the next PWM edge is

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.680
wrong too, and a wrong edge can destroy the bridge.

4
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.380
The usual protection is a software self-test.

5
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.820
It runs periodically, and between runs it sees nothing,

6
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.720
so a fault can act inside that gap.

7
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.100
DG32-LITE carries a second, identical core.

8
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.620
CHECKER runs the same instructions two cycles behind MAIN,

9
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.640
on mirrored inputs and bus responses.

10
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.220
Every value MAIN commits, CHECKER must commit too,

11
00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:37.680
bit for bit.

12
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.440
Now a datapath fault changes one store.

13
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.120
CHECKER commits the right value, and the comparator flags the mismatch on

14
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.100
that store, not at the next self-test.

15
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.520
A latch holds the first cause.

16
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:58.900
FAULT N goes low, and the gate driver switches the bridge off.

17
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.840
In simulation, injection to latch takes 39 cycles.

18
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.400
No firmware is in that path.

19
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.800
Firmware can still prove the path works.

20
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.180
A locked injection register fires it on purpose.

21
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.600
On real silicon, that is the only way to test it.

22
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.680
Safety in the core.

23
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:23.080
DG32-LITE goes to first silicon on the September 2026 shuttle; until then,

24
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.660
every figure here comes from simulation.
